      "/images/catalog/photo-47.webp",
      "/images/catalog/carpet-synt-optimized.webp",
    ],
    slideAlts: [
      "Light geometric area rug on a protected floor, with in-home cleaning equipment",
      "Cleaning a leaf-pattern area rug with a professional extraction wand",
      "Blue patterned area rug in a living room, with a portable Empire machine and extraction wand",
    ],
    heroFit: "contain",
    heroHeight: "70vh",
    intro:
      "Synthetic carpets and area rugs (nylon, polyester, olefin) accumulate oils, grease, and embedded dirt that a vacuum alone cannot remove. Our steam cleaning helps refresh the colors and freshen the fibers directly at your home, without moving the rug.",
    problem:
      "Do your synthetic carpets look dull, sticky, or fade despite vacuuming? It's often a buildup of body oils and soap residue trapped in the fibers — invisible but very real.",
    process: [
      {
        title: "Inspection & Fiber Identification",
        desc: "Our technician begins by precisely identifying the synthetic fiber type: nylon, polyester, olefin, or polypropylene. Each fiber reacts differently — this step determines the exact protocol to apply and avoids any damage to colors or texture.",
      },
      {
        title: "Targeted Degreasing Pre-treatment",
        desc: "We apply a professional degreasing pre-spray on critical areas (walkways, entryways, friction zones). This specialized product penetrates the film of body oil and grease accumulated in synthetic fibers to loosen it before extraction — a step most cleaners skip.",
      },
      {
        title: "Manual Stain Removal",
        desc: "Specific stains (oil, coffee, ink, urine, wine) receive a targeted enzymatic or chemical treatment, chosen from our 40+ professional products. Each stain is worked individually with the right neutralizing agent.",
      },
      {
        title: "Steam Cleaning (Hot Water Extraction)",
        desc: "The main step: our high-pressure equipment injects a hot solution into the fibers and simultaneously vacuums the dirt-laden water. The hot steam helps loosen soil and can reduce bacteria and dust mites in the fibers. The powerful extraction leaves your carpet clean and only slightly damp.",
      },
      {
        title: "Neutralizing Rinse & Fiber Grooming",
        desc: "We finish with a pH-balanced neutralizing rinse that clears cleaning-product residue. Soap residue is the most common reason carpets re-soil quickly. We then manually groom the fibers with a carpet rake for an even finish.",
      },
    ],
    benefits: [
      "Professional degreasing of the oily film",
      "Steam cleaning to help reduce bacteria and dust mites",
      "Neutralizing rinse against sticky residue",
      "Helps bring back shine and softness to synthetic fibers",
      "100% on-site service — no transportation, immediate results",
    ],
    faq: [
      {
        q: "Will you take my synthetic rug to clean it in a warehouse?",
        a: "No, absolutely not. The entire cleaning process is done on-site, in front of you, in your home. This choice is not just a convenience: it is also more effective. We use your water and our high-performance mobile equipment. You see the result immediately.",
      },
      {
        q: "Is steam cleaning safe for synthetic carpets (nylon, polyester)?",
        a: "Yes, provided the right protocol is used — which our certified technicians do. Synthetic fibers tolerate the heat and controlled moisture of our extraction method very well.",
      },
      {
        q: "Why do my synthetic carpets get dirty faster after I clean them myself?",
        a: "This is the classic problem with rental machines and commercial products: they leave detergent residues in the fibers. These residues act like a dirt magnet. Our professional neutralizing rinse clears these residues, which helps the fibers stay cleaner for longer.",
      },
      {
        q: "How long does it take for a synthetic carpet to dry after steam cleaning?",
        a: "Between 2 and 5 hours on average, depending on carpet thickness, fiber type, and room ventilation. Synthetic fibers (nylon, polyester) have the advantage of being naturally hydrophobic — they absorb less water than wool, which speeds up drying. Our powerful extraction removes up to 95% of moisture at the end of the intervention. Opening windows or turning on a fan further reduces this delay.",
      },
      {
        q: "How often should a synthetic carpet be professionally cleaned?",
        a: "For an average household without pets, once a year is ideal. With young children, pets, or a very busy entrance, every 6 to 9 months is recommended. Our technicians observe that clients who wait more than 18 months have carpets that are significantly harder to treat — the accumulated grease polymerizes and adheres to the fibers permanently.",
      },
      {
        q: "Can you remove cooking oil, kitchen grease, or sebum stains on a synthetic carpet?",
        a: "That is precisely the specialty of our treatment for synthetic fibers. Olefin and polypropylene are particularly sensitive to lipid buildup (cooking oils, body sebum). Our professional degreasing pre-treatment is designed to loosen this film before extraction. In many cases, these stains fade noticeably in one visit. For older stains, the improvement can be significant, without a complete removal being assured.",
      },
    ],
    details: `
      <h2 style="color: var(--color-primary); margin-bottom: 1rem;">Expertise for modern synthetic fibers — Nylon, Polyester, Olefin</h2>
      <p>Synthetic carpets and area rugs are the most common in homes across <strong>Montreal</strong>, <strong>Laval</strong>, and the <strong>South Shore</strong>. Nylon, polyester, olefin (polypropylene), and acrylic make up the vast majority of residential floor coverings sold today. These fibers are durable and stain-resistant on the surface, but they hold body oils and grease: an invisible film that dulls colors and can make the surface sticky.</p>
      <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">A vacuum cleaner, no matter how powerful, cannot remove this greasy film. <strong>Synthetic area rug cleaning</strong>, like wall-to-wall synthetic carpet cleaning, uses hot-water extraction — a method often recommended for nylon, polyester and olefin. The heat helps liquefy the greases, the pre-treatment loosens them, and extraction lifts them from the fibers. Colors and softness can be refreshed. Steam can also reduce some bacteria and dust mites in the fibers; the result depends on the fiber, the soil level and the room's airflow.</p>
      <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">With over 30 years of in-home carpet cleaning, our technicians match the protocol to each synthetic fiber. We travel to <strong>Montreal</strong>, <strong>Laval</strong>, <strong>Longueuil</strong>, <strong>Brossard</strong>, <strong>Saint-Lambert</strong>, and across the <strong>South Shore</strong> with our mobile units.</p>
    `,
  },
  {
    slug: "tapis-laine",
    title: "In-Home Wool Rug Cleaning",
    seoTitle: "Wool Rug Cleaning | Montreal & South Shore",
    description:
      "In-home wool area rug cleaning using fibre-appropriate products and methods. Montreal, Laval and the South Shore.",
    category: "Carpets",
    icon: "",
    image: "/images/catalog/photo-39.webp",
    slides: [
      "/images/catalog/photo-39.webp",
      "/images/catalog/wool-steam-optimized.webp",
      "/images/catalog/photo-176.webp",
      "/images/catalog/photo-177.webp",
    ],
    heroFit: "contain",
    heroHeight: "70vh",
    intro:
      "Wool area rug cleaning calls for precise expertise. We perform wool rug cleaning at your home with fibre-appropriate products, and we adapt the precautions to the floor type and the conditions of the visit.",
    problem:
      "A wool rug is a giant filter for your home. It traps an impressive amount of dust, abrasive sand, and allergens. Improper cleaning with overly alkaline products or water that is too hot risks 'cooking' the animal fiber (browning) or causing irreversible shrinkage.",
    process: [
      {
        title: "Inspection and Fiber Testing",
        desc: "We first verify the exact composition of your rug (pure wool, synthetic blend, hand-knotted) and test dye stability before applying anything.",
      },
      {
        title: "Specialized Deep Dry Vacuuming (Dusting)",
        desc: "Wool holds a lot of sand and dry particles at its base that cut fibers over time. Meticulous vacuuming is crucial before any wet treatment.",
      },
      {
        title: "Wool-Safe Pre-treatment (Neutral pH)",
        desc: "Application of a cleaner specifically formulated for animal fibers. It gently dislodges body oils and embedded dirt without opening or damaging the natural scales of the wool.",
      },
      {
        title: "Controlled Extraction (Low Temperature)",
        desc: "We use our high-performance extractor with controlled lukewarm water. Cleaning is done without thermal shock in order to limit the risk of shrinkage or color bleeding.",
      },
      {
        title: "Neutralization and Safe Drying",
        desc: "A final rinse brings the fiber's pH back toward its natural state. Extraction aims to limit moisture in the backing, depending on the floor type and the conditions in the room.",
      },
    ],
    benefits: [
      "Wool-Safe products at a pH suited to animal fibers",
      "A method intended to limit shrinkage and discoloration",
      "Drying precautions based on the floor and the conditions of the visit",
      "Removal of abrasive sand that wears the rug prematurely",
      "Convenient at-home service (no heavy transport required)",
    ],
    faq: [
      {
        q: "Will my wool rug smell like wet sheep after cleaning?",
        a: "Yes, this is completely normal. Pure wool naturally gives off an animal odor when damp — a sign of real natural fiber rather than synthetic. This smell usually fades once the rug dries, often within a few hours.",
      },
      {
        q: "Is it safe to clean my wool rug directly on my hardwood floor?",
        a: "We use controlled-moisture extraction and we avoid soaking the backing. Depending on the floor — hardwood, floating or another type — and the conditions in the room, these precautions aim to limit moisture reaching the floor. They do not guarantee the floor in every situation.",
      },
      {
        q: "What is the difference between cleaning a wool rug and a synthetic rug?",
        a: "The difference is major. Wool is a delicate animal fiber (like hair) that requires a neutral pH (between 5.5 and 7) and lukewarm temperatures. Using synthetic products on wool will chemically burn it (persistent browning) or cause irreversible shrinkage.",
      },
      {
        q: "Can you remove dog or cat urine on a wool rug?",
        a: "Yes, but it requires a very specific enzymatic treatment. Urine is acidic when fresh but becomes very alkaline as it dries, which damages and discolors wool permanently if not treated. We use gentle enzymes that digest uric acid crystals without attacking the animal fiber. The chances of success are high, especially if the accident is recent. Never use alkaline pet store products on wool.",
      },
      {
        q: "Do you offer cleaning for wool rug fringes?",
        a: "Yes, we also clean fringes. Fringes are often made of cotton, which requires a different treatment than the central wool part. We apply a specific cotton cleaner and use a manual technique with a soft brush to restore their whiteness without fraying them.",
      },
      {
        q: "Why is it important to have a wool rug professionally cleaned regularly?",
        a: "The structure of wool (microscopic scales) hides dirt exceptionally well. A wool rug can hold up to its own weight in dust, sand, and allergens before looking visibly dirty. This gritty sand acts like sandpaper every time you walk on it, cutting fibers at the base and causing premature wear (bald spots). An annual professional cleaning eliminates this abrasive sand and prolongs the rug's life.",
      },
    ],
    details: `
      <h2 style="color: var(--color-primary); margin-bottom: 1rem;">Wool-Safe Expertise for your natural fibers in Montreal and Laval</h2>
      <p>Wool is arguably the most luxurious and durable fiber for a residential rug, but it is also the least forgiving during cleaning. Typical mistakes by non-specialized companies or rental machines (overly alkaline products, boiling water, over-oxygenation) often result in fiber browning (chemical cooking phenomenon), color loss (bleeding), or disastrous shrinkage.</p>
      <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">At <strong>Empire Cleaning Group</strong>, our technicians match the protocol to the wool. We use <strong>Wool-Safe</strong> formulas at a pH suited to animal fiber. Extraction is meant to lift soil while limiting moisture in the backing. In <strong>Montreal</strong>, <strong>Laval</strong>, and on the <strong>South Shore</strong>, this wool area rug cleaning is done in your home.</p>
    `,
  },
  {
    slug: "tapis-orientaux",
    title: "In-Home Persian and Oriental Rug Cleaning",
    seoTitle: "Persian & Oriental Rug Cleaning | Montreal & South Shore",
    description:
      "In-home Persian and Oriental rug cleaning with fibre-specific methods and colour testing before treatment. Montreal and the South Shore.",
    category: "Carpets",
    icon: "",
    image: "/images/catalog/photo-oriental-custom.webp",
    slides: [
      "/images/catalog/photo-oriental-custom.webp",
      "/images/catalog/photo-178.webp",
      "/images/catalog/photo-179.webp",
      "/images/catalog/photo-180.webp",
      "/images/catalog/photo-181.webp",
    ],
    slideAlts: [
      "Persian-style patterned rug in a living room, between a light sofa and a glass coffee table",
      "Red and blue medallion rug with fringe, laid in front of a sofa",
      "Close-up of rolled floral rugs, showing the back and the fringe",
      "Blue-green fringed rug, partly rolled, in sunlight",
      "Navy medallion rug with fringe, seen up close on a wood floor",
    ],
    heroFit: "contain",
    heroHeight: "70vh",
    intro:
      "Persian rug cleaning and Oriental rug cleaning at home begin with a colour test. Hand-knotted rug cleaning stays in your home when that test allows it. Very fragile pieces, including some silk rugs or unstable dyes, may need a different method.",
    problem:
      "The industry is full of horror stories of oriental rugs sent to a 'factory' that return months later discolored, shrunk, or worse, never return at all. Furthermore, the natural (vegetable) dyes used in real Persian rugs are extremely unstable (color bleeding). Cleaning with too high a pH will cause red or blue colors to bleed permanently into light patterns and fringes.",
    process: [
      {
        title: "Colorfastness Test",
        desc: "The most critical step. We test each color before any cleaning. The result decides the method: in-home wet cleaning, a more limited approach, or no wet cleaning if the dyes are too unstable. The test lowers the risk of bleeding; it does not remove that risk in every case.",
      },
      {
        title: "Specialized Dry Vacuuming (Dusting)",
        desc: "Hand-knotted rugs have very tight knots that trap abrasive sand. We perform expert deep vacuuming to eliminate these dry particles that cut the base of the rug before applying anything wet.",
      },
      {
        title: "Application of Color Stabilizers",
        desc: "If the test shows moderate instability, we may apply a stabilizing agent, then a pH-suited shampoo for wool or silk, by hand. If the colors remain too unstable, we do not proceed with wet cleaning.",
      },
      {
        title: "Very Low Moisture Extraction",
        desc: "We adapt the water pressure and suction to the weave. Cleaning follows the direction of the pile in order to respect the shine and texture.",
      },
      {
        title: "Fringe Adjustment and Floor Protection",
        desc: "Cotton fringes receive a separate, gentle treatment. The rug stays in place. Protections aim to limit moisture reaching the floor, depending on the floor type and the conditions in the room.",
      },
    ],
    benefits: [
      "In-home cleaning when the colour test allows it",
      "Dye-fastness test before the method is chosen",
      "A distinct protocol for wool, silk and vegetable dyes",
      "Cleaning with the pile, without harsh alkaline products",
      "Precautions to limit moisture reaching the floor",
    ],
    faq: [
      {
        q: "Why do you clean oriental rugs on-site instead of in a workshop?",
        a: "When the colour test allows it, cleaning is done in your home: you see the products used and the rug does not leave the house. This is not possible for every piece. An unfavorable test can lead to a more limited method, or to postponing wet cleaning.",
      },
      {
        q: "Are the colors of my Persian rug at risk of bleeding?",
        a: "The risk exists if the method is unsuitable, especially with overly alkaline products or water that is too hot on vegetable dyes. We test dye fastness first. Depending on the result, we adapt the method, limit it, or skip wet cleaning. The test lowers the risk; it does not remove it in every case.",
      },
      {
        q: "My oriental rug is hand-knotted, will your big machines damage it?",
        a: "We do not use aggressive rotary brushes on hand-knotted rugs. Our technicians use manual tools and clean in the direction of the pile in order to limit the risk of damaging the knots or reversing the texture.",
      },
      {
        q: "Do you clean rugs that contain silk?",
        a: "Not always. Silk loses strength when wet. Some silk pieces, or rugs with unstable dyes, are not suited to in-home wet cleaning. When the test allows it, we use a very low-moisture method and watched drying, including on rugs that combine wool and silk.",
      },
      {
        q: "How do you clean the white fringes of my rug?",
        a: "The fringes are an extension of the rug's foundation, usually cotton, and they are not treated like the wool in the center. We use a cotton-suited product and a soft hand brush to ease greying and limit fraying.",
      },
      {
        q: "Will you damage my hardwood floor underneath the rug during the intervention?",
        a: "We limit moisture during extraction and avoid soaking the backing. Depending on the floor — hardwood, engineered or another type — and the conditions in the room, these precautions aim to reduce water reaching the floor. They do not guarantee the floor in every situation.",
      },
    ],
    details: `
      <h2 style="color: var(--color-primary); margin-bottom: 1rem;">The truth about cleaning Oriental and Persian rugs</h2>
      <p>Many services offer to take a Persian, Turkish, Afghan or Chinese rug to a workshop for weeks. In-home cleaning is often possible in <strong>Montreal</strong>, <strong>Laval</strong> and on the <strong>South Shore</strong>, after the colour test. Not every piece is suited to it.</p>
      <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">The main risk with a hand-knotted rug comes from vegetable dyes, which are often unstable. The test chooses the method: low moisture, a more limited approach, or no wet cleaning if silk or the dyes will not allow it. We clean with the pile, using pH-suited products, and you can see the work when it takes place in your home.</p>
    `,
  },
];

export function getStaticPaths() {
  // tapis-residentiel and tapis-commercial are no longer published: 301 to the SEO pages
  const slugs = ["tapis-synthetique", "tapis-laine", "tapis-orientaux"];
  return slugs.map((slug) => ({ params: { service: slug } }));
}

const { service: serviceParam } = Astro.params;
const service = services.find((s) => s.slug === serviceParam);

if (!service) {
  return Astro.redirect("/404");
}
---

<Layout
  title={
    service.slug === "tapis-synthetique" ||
    service.slug === "tapis-laine" ||
    service.slug === "tapis-orientaux"
      ? service.seoTitle
      : `${service.seoTitle} | Empire Cleaning Group`
  }
  description={service.description ?? service.intro}
  image={service.image}
>
  <style define:vars={{ heroHeight: service.heroHeight || "auto" }}>
    .service-hero {
      min-height: var(--heroHeight);
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
    }
  </style>

  <!-- SPLIT SCREEN HERO -->
  <div class="split-hero-container mobile-hero-stack">
    <div class="container full-width-split">
      <div class="split-layout">
        <!-- LEFT COLUMN: CONTENT (Hook) -->
        <div class="split-content fade-in-section">
          <div class="service-hero-intro">
          <span class="service-cat-badge">{service.category}</span>
          <h1 class="split-title fade-in-up text-gradient-empire">
            {service.title}
          </h1>

          <div class="mobile-hero-slideshow fade-in-up delay-050">
            {
              service.slides && service.slides.length > 1 ? (
                <div class="slideshow-container">
                  {service.slides.map((slide, index) => (
                    <img
                      src={slide}
                      alt={service.slideAlts?.[index] ?? `${service.title} - Image ${index + 1}`}
                      class={`slide ${index === 0 ? "active" : ""}`}
                      loading={index === 0 ? "eager" : "lazy"}
                    />
                  ))}
                </div>
              ) : (
                <div class="slideshow-container">
                  <img
                    src={service.image}
                    alt={service.title}
                    class="slide active"
                    loading="eager"
                  />
                </div>
              )
            }
            <div class="image-overlay-gradient"></div>
          </div>

          <div class="problem-statement fade-in-up delay-100">
            <h2 class="problem-title">The Problem:</h2>
            <p class="problem-text">{service.problem}</p>
          </div>

          <p class="lead-text fade-in-up delay-200">{service.intro}</p>

          </div>

          <div class="service-hero-action-band">

          <ConversionCtaPriorityGroup locale="en" buttonStyle="primary" />
          {service.slug === "tapis-laine" && (
            <ServiceVideoProof
              title="See Our Rug Cleaning in Action"
              subtitle="Professional wool rug cleaning at home"
              description="Watch a real in-home cleaning and see our professional steam cleaning method for delicate and high-quality rugs."
              youtubeId="6GDp9ff_6EY"
              youtubeTitle="Oriental Rug Cleaning at Home in Westmount | Steam Cleaning"
              allVideosHref="/en/video-gallery/"
              posterUrl="/images/videos/video-thumb-tapis-oriental-new-v2.webp"
              videoAspectRatio="9 / 16"
              playLabel="Watch the Video"
              allVideosLabel="See All Our Work"
              closeLabel="Close video"
            />
          )}
          {service.slug === "tapis-orientaux" && (
            <ServiceVideoProof
              title="See Our Rug Cleaning in Action"
              subtitle="Oriental rug cleaning at home"
              description="Watch a real in-home cleaning and see our professional steam cleaning method for delicate and high-quality rugs."
              youtubeId="6GDp9ff_6EY"
              youtubeTitle="Oriental Rug Cleaning at Home in Westmount | Steam Cleaning"
              allVideosHref="/en/video-gallery/"
              posterUrl="/images/videos/video-thumb-tapis-oriental-new-v2.webp"
              videoAspectRatio="9 / 16"
              playLabel="Watch the Video"
              allVideosLabel="See All Our Work"
              closeLabel="Close video"
            />
          )}
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- RIGHT COLUMN: IMAGE (Visual) -->
        <div class="split-image-wrapper fade-in-section delay-200">
          {
            service.slides && service.slides.length > 1 ? (
              <div class="slideshow-container">
                {service.slides.map((slide, index) => (
                  <img
                    src={slide}
                    alt={service.slideAlts?.[index] ?? `${service.title} - Image ${index + 1}`}
                    class={`slide ${index === 0 ? "active" : ""}`}
                    loading={index === 0 ? "eager" : "lazy"}
                  />
                ))}
              </div>
            ) : (
              <img
                src={service.image}
                alt={service.title}
                class="split-image"
                draggable="false"
              />
            )
          }
          <div class="image-overlay-gradient"></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="service-layout">
        <!-- MAIN CONTENT -->
        <div class="service-main">
          <!-- PROCESS -->
